Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Period live-action. A fifth-floor Milwaukee walk-up: worn linoleum, brown chair, pill bottles in a straight line. NARRATOR, a slight young man in a pizza-stained jacket, watches his broad older brother NICK haul grocery bags.

(SOUND: grocery bags thump onto the kitchen counter; radiator hisses)

NARRATOR V.O.
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

NARRATOR V.O.
His face looked like a puzzle held together with sticky stuff from a glue gun.

NICK, broad-shouldered, reddish-brown hair uncombed, scars on his chin and left eyebrow, takes six bags without strain.

NARRATOR V.O.
He wasn’t ugly. Just had a few brawls over the years.

MA, OLIVIA OLSEN, gray-haired and frail in a faded housecoat, moves a pill bottle from the front of the line to the back.

MA
Did we go to church today?

NARRATOR
No, Ma. Your pills are right here.

Nick studies Ma, then turns on NARRATOR.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day.

NICK
That’s how old people go downhill.

NARRATOR
I’ll do better.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick was right. He was always right. Or I thought he was.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

A brick corner gym. Through the window, a muscular regular abandons a curl bar when Nick enters.

(SOUND: weights clang against rubber flooring; a jump rope snaps)

NARRATOR, in a loose gray T-shirt, follows Nick between the benches.

NARRATOR V.O.
When Nick walked in the gym, people took notice. Man, did they move out of the way.

Nick curls a heavy bar, easy and expressionless.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick was my brother. I knew he would stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR parks his battered bicycle outside a neighborhood pizza parlor. SIONNA, pale and slender with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a backpack, trips on broken cement into him.

(SOUND: bicycle chain clicks; traffic washes past)

NARRATOR catches her elbow.

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes dust from her shirt. A small fish symbol hangs from her necklace.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My parents named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

NARRATOR stares into her pale blue eyes.

NARRATOR
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza.

SIONNA
But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

(SOUND: a distant gull cries; the pizzeria doorbell jingles)

NARRATOR V.O.
It was like I knew her. Like I had seen her somewhere before.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

A Packers game flickers over a smoky bar. NICK, in a brown suede jacket and work boots, drops onto the stool beside NARRATOR.

(SOUND: football announcer muffled by bar chatter; pool balls crack)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

NARRATOR
Ma got confused today. She couldn’t find her pills.

Nick’s jaw tightens.

NICK
Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

NICK
Do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart.

NARRATOR looks down at his beer.

NARRATOR
I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK
That’s more like it.

NARRATOR leaves money on the bar and crosses toward the street.

EXT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

NARRATOR pauses outside the old library’s tall glowing windows. He grips a library-card application and steps inside.

(SOUND: winter wind rattles the doors; a clock chimes faintly)

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Old wood floors and brass lamps stretch between towering shelves. LIBRARIAN ELLEN, silver-haired and neat in a cardigan, stamps a temporary card.

(SOUND: book cart wheels squeak; pages rustle)

LIBRARIAN ELLEN
Your plastic card will be mailed within the week.

NARRATOR scans the classic literature shelves, pulls out WUTHERING HEIGHTS, and holds it like contraband.

NARRATOR V.O.
Who needed school when you could get anything in here for free?

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A small bedroom with a narrow bed and a paperback open beneath a weak lamp. NARRATOR reads WUTHERING HEIGHTS as Ma’s television murmurs through the wall.

(SOUND: television game-show applause; pages turn)

NARRATOR V.O.
It was about a guy named Heathcliff and a girl named Catherine.

NARRATOR V.O.
I got the feeling these two really loved each other.

NARRATOR closes the book. Sionna’s number lies beside it.

NARRATOR V.O.
It reminded me of Sionna.

EXT. WOODLAND FIELD - DREAM - DAY

NARRATOR, now in an embroidered red tunic and brown hose, rides behind SIONNA on a dappled mare through lush green woods. Her brown hair falls to her waist.

(SOUND: horse hooves soften in moss; birdsong floats through leaves)

They enter a sunlit field. NARRATOR helps Sionna down, his white linen sleeves billowing.

NARRATOR V.O.
I knew that girl. It was Sionna, but in a different place.

He kisses her. Their blue-eyed gaze locks.

(MUSIC: warm strings swell, then warp into a low pulse)

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR bolts upright, sweating in the cold room.

(SOUND: his breath pounds; an old pipe knocks in the wall)

NARRATOR
Hold!

NARRATOR V.O.
What the hell was that?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

A crowded coffee shop full of backpacks, textbooks, and steam. SIONNA sits at a back table in a loose green hooded top. NARRATOR approaches with WUTHERING HEIGHTS tucked under his arm.

(SOUND: espresso machine hisses; cups clink)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl.

NARRATOR
They grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
Yeah. I read it.

NARRATOR winces at himself. Sionna smiles.

SIONNA
The moors are like the people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

SIONNA
Heathcliff’s love never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR absorbs this.

NARRATOR
A love that never changes will destroy you.

SIONNA
Maybe. We have to be willing to let go of things.

(SOUND: a spoon circles porcelain; rain taps the window)

NARRATOR
I had a dream about you. A horse, a forest, old clothes.

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

NARRATOR V.O.
Something inside of me was coming awake.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR struggles on a purple mat among quiet students. SIONNA moves easily beside him, barefoot and calm.

(SOUND: measured breathing; floorboards creak)

NARRATOR’S shirt falls over his face in downward dog. He tucks it into his shorts, embarrassed.

SIONNA
You’re really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice.

SIONNA
Pretty soon you won’t notice anyone else or what they’re doing.

NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

They smile.

(MUSIC: a soft sustained bowl tone fades into silence)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma sits in her brown chair beneath her afghan. Nick stands over NARRATOR, fists opening and closing.

(SOUND: game-show audience cheers from the television; wall clock ticks)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass.

NICK
Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? With who?

NARRATOR
I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That’s all.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

NARRATOR
I said I was sorry!

Nick slams the apartment door behind him.

(SOUND: door slams; Ma’s teacup rattles in its saucer)

NARRATOR watches Ma’s blank face.

NARRATOR V.O.
God, sometimes I really hated Nick inside.

INT. DOCTOR CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

Ma sits pale and dizzy on an exam table. NARRATOR waits by the window as DR. CARSON, a tired middle-aged physician, reviews her chart.

(SOUND: blood pressure cuff deflates; distant ambulance siren)

DR. CARSON
Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high.

DR. CARSON
I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke.

NARRATOR
She’s been taking her pills. I make sure.

DR. CARSON
She needs the emergency room. She will be admitted for observation.

Ma presses a hand to her chest.

MA
Someone must be talking about me.

NARRATOR calls from the wall phone, then waits with Ma as Nick’s burnt-orange Trans Am pulls up outside.

(SOUND: phone dial clicks; Trans Am engine rumbles)

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - NIGHT

Ma sleeps in a hospital bed. NARRATOR dozes in a chair, WUTHERING HEIGHTS open on his lap. Nick’s silhouette rises in NARRATOR’S dream across a battlefield.

(SOUND: monitor beeps; dream swords clash beneath the hospital hum)

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DREAM - NIGHT

Mud, steel, and bodies. NARRATOR, in a torn tunic, kneels beside a dying soldier.

(SOUND: men scream; blades strike shields)

DYING SOLDIER
Save her.

NICK charges through smoke in dark armor, a knife raised.

NARRATOR
Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK
That you are, little brother.

Nick drives the blade into NARRATOR’S chest.

(SOUND: blade pierces cloth; battlefield sound drops into a deep ringing)

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - NIGHT

NARRATOR wakes gasping. Ma dozes peacefully beside him.

(SOUND: monitor beeps return; NARRATOR’S chair scrapes)

NARRATOR V.O.
Why would Nick want to kill me?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma is home again. A blue weekly pill organizer rests on the coffee table. SIONNA kneels beside Ma, explaining the labeled compartments. Nick enters, wild-haired and red-faced.

(SOUND: pillbox lids snap shut; television detective show murmurs)

MA
You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me.

NICK
Man, you shoulda been a nurse.

NARRATOR
Knock it off. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track.

NICK picks up the pill organizer and smirks.

NICK
Aren’t you Nurse Nancy.

NARRATOR
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick! You haven’t even met her.

NICK
Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

NARRATOR
I’m not a bastard!

NICK slaps NARRATOR across the head.

(SOUND: sharp slap; NARRATOR’S ear rings)

NICK
Don’t you ever talk that way about my mother.

Nick storms out.

(SOUND: door slams; Ma whimpers softly)

Sionna holds NARRATOR’S hand as he steadies himself.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Nick drinks with BOB and SAMMY near the pool tables. NARRATOR sits apart, tense. LINDSEY, blonde and guarded, stands with her dark-haired boyfriend DAVID.

(SOUND: AC/DC blares; pool balls crack; glasses clink)

NICK
Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

NARRATOR
Why does it matter? You broke up with her.

NICK crosses to Lindsey and David.

NICK
Where did you meet this one?

DAVID
What the fuck is wrong with you?

Nick’s fists clench. The pool room falls still.

(SOUND: music continues under sudden silence; a cue stick rolls across felt)

NARRATOR steps directly before Nick.

NARRATOR
Nick, listen. You don’t have any problem with this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it.

NARRATOR
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick swings. NARRATOR recoils as Joe and BOUNCER HARRIS rush in.

(SOUND: fist whooshes past; tables scrape; Lindsey cries out)

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

Nick head-butts Harris and tears free. NARRATOR runs out into the night.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

NARRATOR races up the steps, breath steaming. Sionna opens the door in a loose sweater and pulls him inside.

(SOUND: NARRATOR’S ragged breathing; a neighbor’s dog barks)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A green suede sofa, books, candles, and Sionna’s gray cat. NARRATOR curls against Sionna’s lap.

(SOUND: candle flame flickers; rain begins against glass)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I love Nick, though. He’s my brother.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

SIONNA
You can’t let him intimidate you or stop you from your purpose.

NARRATOR
My purpose. What is my purpose?

SIONNA cups his face.

SIONNA
Find the true self within you, and honor it.

SIONNA
Be the best person you can while you’re here.

(MUSIC: gentle piano under rain)

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Weeks later. Nick’s Werner Street apartment is wrecked with trash, empty cups, and clothes. NARRATOR stands in the doorway, wary. Nick is unshaven, still broad but rumpled in a stained T-shirt.

(SOUND: microwave hum; traffic groans below)

NICK
You’ve ruined my life, man.

NICK
People are saying my little brother tried to kick my ass.

NARRATOR
I didn’t do anything. I just didn’t want you to hurt that guy.

NICK
You’re some hot shit college kid now.

NARRATOR
I’m working my ass off and finding out how stupid I am.

Nick sits, then points at NARRATOR.

NICK
You are my little brother. I’ll let it slide this time.

NICK
But don’t you ever interrupt me in public again.

NARRATOR
Will you see Ma? She’s dying to see you.

NICK
Yeah. Tell her I’ve been sick or something.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - EVENING

Sionna serves peach pie. Ma laughs as she tells a story. Nick enters in his suede jacket, then stops when Sionna offers her hand.

(SOUND: forks scrape plates; television volume low)

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. You must be Nick.

NICK
Uh, yeah. Likewise.

SIONNA
You’re just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

NICK looks Sionna over.

NICK
I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
That’s right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
I’m thinking college professor.

SIONNA
It will be another year of work before I can call myself a graduate.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off.

SIONNA
There are more females in college today than males.

SIONNA
You can’t keep a good woman down, so to speak.

(SOUND: Ma’s fork stops against her plate; a tense silence)

NICK
Women can’t stay home and take care of their kids.

SIONNA
Women bring different ways of thinking to the table.

SIONNA
Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to make a difference.

Nick rises, his face burning red.

NICK
Take care of this numbskull.

He grips NARRATOR’S shoulder, then whispers close.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

NARRATOR
You know I will.

EXT. WERNER STREET INTERSECTION - NIGHT

Nick’s burnt-orange Trans Am, flames painted down its sides, drives Ma home from mass. Streetlights reflect on wet pavement.

(SOUND: windshield wipers sweep; low engine growl)

MA
It was nice to go to church.

NICK
Yeah, Ma.

Nick’s hand closes around a Jack and Coke in the console.

(SOUND: ice shifts in the glass; tires hiss on wet asphalt)

Headlights blast across the intersection.

(SOUND: violent metal impact; glass explodes; horn blares without stopping)

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

NARRATOR enters the silent apartment. Ma’s chair is empty. The answering machine’s red light blinks.

(SOUND: refrigerator hum; answering-machine beep)

OFFICER DUPUIS V.O.
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department.

OFFICER DUPUIS V.O.
We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NARRATOR rewinds, shaking, and calls.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

NARRATOR sits across from OFFICER DUPUIS, a large man with graying hair and a thin mustache.

(SOUND: phones ring; fluorescent lights buzz)

OFFICER DUPUIS
There has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother.

NARRATOR
Is Ma okay?

OFFICER DUPUIS lowers his eyes.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your mother passed away at the scene.

NARRATOR goes still.

(SOUND: paper shifts; NARRATOR’S plastic water cup trembles)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NARRATOR stands before Ma’s empty brown chair. His hands claw at his hair. Sionna enters and holds him as he collapses.

(SOUND: NARRATOR sobs; wall clock ticks relentlessly)

NARRATOR
He fucking killed my mom.

NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die.

SIONNA
Let it out. We’ll figure it all out.

(MUSIC: low grieving cello)

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - ICU - DAY

Nick lies bruised beneath white sheets, oxygen tubing in his nose, a chest tube draining into a plastic box. NARRATOR stands at his bedside in funeral-black clothes.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeps; oxygen hisses)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man.

NICK
I can’t believe Ma’s gone.

NARRATOR
The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK
I had three Jack and Cokes before I remembered I needed to take her to church.

NICK
The cops say vehicular manslaughter.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick’s face hardens despite his tears.

NICK
You think I meant to kill her?

NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

NARRATOR turns and leaves.

(SOUND: monitor beeps accelerate, then settle; ICU door clicks shut)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Months later. Sionna’s left shoulder is braced beneath a loose shirt. Her blue eyes are dull. NARRATOR sits opposite her, exhausted.

(SOUND: rain needles the window; a kettle clicks off)

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR kneels before her.

NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.

SIONNA
Maybe I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

NARRATOR
Please stay. We can work things out.

SIONNA looks down at her hands.

SIONNA
I need space.

(MUSIC: a lone piano note repeats beneath the rain)

NARRATOR V.O.
My exit from the house was a free fall into a black, endless void.

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DAY

Years later. NARRATOR, older, stronger, neatly dressed in a dark coat, walks through gray Seattle rain toward Pike Place. His phone shows a photo of twelve TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA locations.

(SOUND: ferry horn; rain on awnings; market vendors call)

NARRATOR V.O.
Many years had passed since Milwaukee.

NARRATOR V.O.
Thanks to Tony, I did pretty well for myself in the pizza business.

NARRATOR enters a coffee shop. CHRIS, a blonde barista with a sloppy bun and hoop earrings, smiles over the counter.

CHRIS
The usual coffee?

NARRATOR
Yeah. Simple is good.

CHRIS
You look like you’ve got somewhere to be.

NARRATOR
Milwaukee. I have to see my brother.

CHRIS
That sounds big.

NARRATOR
It is.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - VISITATION ROOM - DAY

NARRATOR sits behind thick plexiglass with a yellow phone. Across from him, Nick is older, scrawny, graying, and lined beneath his prison uniform.

(SOUND: locked doors clank; fluorescent lights hum; distant phones murmur)

NICK lifts the receiver.

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK
It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

NICK
Maybe I get out in two more years.

NARRATOR takes a breath.

NARRATOR
I need you to listen to me.

NICK
Okay, man.

NARRATOR
You killed my mother in a drunk-driving accident.

NARRATOR
You held me down my entire life, forcing me to take care of Ma.

NARRATOR
You tried to kill the love of my life.

Nick drops his eyes.

(SOUND: a guard’s keys jangle; NARRATOR’S breath fills the receiver)

NARRATOR
Frankly, you’re an asshole.

NARRATOR
But I forgive you.

Nick looks up, stunned.

NARRATOR
I forgive you because it sets me free.

NARRATOR
It releases your control over me forever.

Tears move through the bruised memory of Nick’s face.

NICK
I’m so sorry.

NICK
You’re right, man. I did all that shit.

NICK
You’re more than I ever was. You’re a big man, and I’m a small one.

NARRATOR
I still love you for being my brother.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better.

NICK
What happened with you?

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’ll be expanding.

NARRATOR
You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK smiles through tears.

NICK
President Olson. That’s fucking amazing.

NICK
I am so proud of you, man.

(SOUND: guard taps the glass; metal door unlocks)

GUARD HARRIS
Time is up.

NICK
Bye.

NARRATOR hangs up and watches Nick disappear down the corridor.

EXT. MILWAUKEE MITCHELL AIRPORT - DAY

NARRATOR walks toward security with a carry-on bag. Autumn sun breaks briefly through clouds. He checks his boarding pass, then smiles at his own name.

(SOUND: rolling suitcase wheels; airport announcement; distant jet engines)

NICK V.O.
President Olson.

NARRATOR
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

NARRATOR walks toward the gate without looking back.

(MUSIC: quiet hopeful strings rise)

FADE OUT.
